<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jetpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1760 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Jetpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kathi state of central Saurashtra, held by branches of the Vala Kathi clan that also ruled Bilkha.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jetpur came under the Vala branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathi-people">Kathis</a>, a pastoral warrior community that gave its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a>, around 1760. The holding was divided among several shareholders of the same family.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathi-people">Kathi Darbars</a> governed Jetpur and its villages under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar-agency">Kathiawar Agency</a>, with land shared between branches in a way typical of Kathi tenure. The state was minor, and little detail of its administration is recorded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Jetpur was merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saurashtra-state">United State of Saurashtra</a> in 1948. The town, now in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rajkot-state">Rajkot</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, is known for textile dyeing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1760 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.73%;width:3.74%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st